------------------------------------------------------------
18. strcpy() DANGER
------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

char source[] = "Hello";
char destination[3];

strcpy(destination, source);


There is insufficient space.

This causes undefined behavior.

Always ensure the destination object is large enough.


------------------------------------------------------------
19. strncpy()
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

char source[] = "Hello";
char destination[10];

strncpy(destination, source, sizeof destination);


`strncpy()` has subtle semantics.

It does NOT always guarantee null termination.

Example:

char destination[5];

strncpy(destination, "Hello", sizeof destination);


There may be no '\0' in destination.

Therefore, do not assume strncpy() always produces a valid
C string.


------------------------------------------------------------
20. SAFER STRING COPY PATTERN
------------------------------------------------------------

If you control the destination size, one approach is:

size_t n = sizeof destination;

if (n > 0)
{
    strncpy(destination, source, n - 1);
    destination[n - 1] = '\0';
}


This guarantees termination when n > 0.

However, this may truncate the source string.

Production code should define what truncation means.


